 such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{{field-1}}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}{{field-2}},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the \n{{field-3}} \nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/27/27e921a76383c4f8a4fc1e81a1febcfad53f330d0e902f4e07578238da33422f.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.07$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.14$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/36/36ae671944545eaae9027ef0d4a876e12ceef17d01be0ad881d32dfdcb8ad963.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fd20f7333800323c44f41d7906958a0928d5f30279e36b1f50d0ca1f66fcd46.png)"] [17592186551347 "$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+1, & x\\in [-3,0) \\\\ {x}^{2}+1, & x\\in [0,3)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous